---
/**
 * Marginal relief above Rs 12 lakh, worked through with the engine's own numbers. The band's upper
 * end, every table row and the chart are computed at build time, so they cannot drift from the
 * calculator.
 */
import DocLayout from '../layouts/DocLayout.astro';
import { formatRupees } from '../lib/format';
import { calculate } from '../lib/tax/calculate';
import { DEFAULT_INPUT } from '../lib/tax/defaults';
import { computeIncomeTax } from '../lib/tax/income-tax';
import { CESS_RATE, REBATE } from '../lib/tax/rates';

const LIMIT = REBATE.new.incomeLimit;
const tax = (income: number) => computeIncomeTax('new', income);
const withoutRelief = (income: number) =>
  income <= LIMIT ? 0 : tax(income).slabTax * (1 + CESS_RATE);

// Where the taper stops binding: the first income above the limit at which the ordinary slab tax
// is no more than the income above the limit.
let low = LIMIT;
let high = LIMIT + 2_00_000;
while (high - low > 1) {
  const mid = Math.floor((low + high) / 2);
  if (tax(mid).rebateIsMarginalRelief) low = mid;
  else high = mid;
}
const bandEnd = low;
const worstKeep = bandEnd - tax(bandEnd).totalTax;
const worstLoss = LIMIT - worstKeep;
const cliff = withoutRelief(LIMIT + 1);

const TAXABLE_ROWS = [
  11_90_000,
  12_00_000,
  12_00_001,
  12_10_000,
  12_25_000,
  12_50_000,
  12_60_000,
  bandEnd,
  12_75_000,
  12_90_000,
  13_00_000,
];

// The same band in CTC terms, using the calculator's standard structure.
const CTC_ROWS = [13_00_000, 13_25_000, 13_50_000, 13_75_000, 14_00_000, 14_25_000, 14_50_000].map(
  (ctc) => {
    const r = calculate({ ...DEFAULT_INPUT, ctc });
    return {
      ctc,
      taxable: r.regimes.new.taxableIncome,
      tax: r.regimes.new.tax.totalTax,
      relief: r.regimes.new.tax.rebateIsMarginalRelief,
      monthly: r.regimes.new.monthlyTakeHome,
    };
  },
);

// Chart: what you keep (taxable income less tax) from Rs 11.8 lakh to Rs 13 lakh.
const W = 640;
const H = 260;
const PAD = { left: 64, right: 16, top: 16, bottom: 36 };
const X0 = 11_80_000;
const X1 = 13_00_000;
const Y0 = 11_30_000;
const Y1 = 12_30_000;
const x = (v: number) => PAD.left + ((v - X0) / (X1 - X0)) * (W - PAD.left - PAD.right);
const y = (v: number) => PAD.top + (1 - (v - Y0) / (Y1 - Y0)) * (H - PAD.top - PAD.bottom);
const points = (keep: (income: number) => number) => {
  const out: string[] = [];
  for (let v = X0; v <= X1; v += 2_500) {
    // Draw the drop at the limit as a vertical step rather than a slope.
    if (v === LIMIT + 2_500)
      out.push(`${x(LIMIT + 1).toFixed(1)},${y(keep(LIMIT + 1)).toFixed(1)}`);
    out.push(`${x(v).toFixed(1)},${y(keep(v)).toFixed(1)}`);
  }
  return out.join(' ');
};
const keptWith = points((v) => v - tax(v).totalTax);
const keptWithout = points((v) => v - withoutRelief(v));
const lakh = (v: number) => `₹${(v / 1_00_000).toFixed(v % 1_00_000 === 0 ? 0 : 1)}L`;
---

<DocLayout
  title="Marginal relief above ₹12 lakh"
  description="How the section 156 rebate and marginal relief work just above ₹12 lakh of taxable income in FY 2026-27, worked out to the rupee, including the 4% cess most explanations leave out."
  updated="2026-09-30"
  ogImage="marginal-relief"
  about={['Marginal relief', 'Section 156 rebate', 'Income tax in India']}
>
  <p>
    Under the new regime, the section 156 rebate (the old section 87A) cancels the tax on taxable
    income up to {formatRupees(LIMIT)}. Without anything else, crossing that line by a single rupee
    would take the rebate away entirely, and a salary with {formatRupees(LIMIT + 1)} of taxable
    income would owe <strong>{formatRupees(cliff)}</strong> in tax and cess. Marginal relief exists
    so that this cliff never happens.
  </p>

  <h2>What the relief does</h2>
  <p>
    Section 156(2)(b) of the Income-tax Act, 2025 caps the income-tax at the amount by which your
    taxable income exceeds {formatRupees(LIMIT)}. At {formatRupees(12_10_000)} of taxable income,
    the ordinary slab tax would be {formatRupees(tax(12_10_000).slabTax)}; the relief cuts it to{' '}
    {formatRupees(tax(12_10_000).taxAfterRebate)}, the income above the limit.
  </p>
  <p>
    The relief stops mattering once the ordinary slab tax falls below the income above the limit.
    That happens at <strong>{formatRupees(bandEnd)}</strong> of taxable income. Above it, you simply
    pay the slab tax.
  </p>

  <h2>The part most explanations leave out: the 4% cess</h2>
  <p>
    The health and education cess is charged on the tax after the rebate, so it applies to the
    capped amount too. Inside the band, every extra rupee of taxable income therefore costs ₹1.04:
    the rupee itself, plus 4 paise of cess. You are never hit by the cliff, but you are not quite
    made whole either. At the top of the band you keep <strong>{formatRupees(worstLoss)}</strong>{' '}
    less than someone with exactly {formatRupees(LIMIT)} of taxable income.
  </p>

  <figure class="my-8">
    <svg
      viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${H}`}
      class="w-full"
      role="img"
      aria-label={`Income kept after tax, from ${lakh(X0)} to ${lakh(X1)} of taxable income. Without relief it drops by ${formatRupees(cliff)} at ${lakh(LIMIT)}. With relief it dips by at most ${formatRupees(worstLoss)}.`}
    >
      {[11_40_000, 11_70_000, 12_00_000, 12_30_000].map((v) => (
        <g>
          <line
            x1={PAD.left}
            x2={W - PAD.right}
            y1={y(v)}
            y2={y(v)}
            stroke="var(--color-line)"
            stroke-width="1"
          />
          <text
            x={PAD.left - 8}
            y={y(v) + 4}
            text-anchor="end"
            font-size="12"
            fill="var(--color-ink-3)"
          >
            {lakh(v)}
          </text>
        </g>
      ))}
      {[11_80_000, 12_00_000, 12_20_000, 12_40_000, 12_60_000, 12_80_000, 13_00_000].map((v) => (
        <text x={x(v)} y={H - 12} text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="var(--color-ink-3)">
          {lakh(v)}
        </text>
      ))}
      <polyline
        points={keptWithout}
        fill="none"
        stroke="var(--color-chart-tax)"
        stroke-width="2"
        stroke-dasharray="5 4"
      />
      <polyline points={keptWith} fill="none" stroke="var(--color-accent)" stroke-width="2" />
      <text x={x(12_62_000)} y={y(12_10_000)} font-size="13" fill="var(--color-accent-ink)">
        With relief
      </text>
      <text x={x(12_05_000)} y={y(11_40_000)} font-size="13" fill="var(--color-ink-2)">
        Without relief
      </text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-3">
      Income kept after tax and cess, by taxable income under the new regime. The table below has
      the exact figures.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Taxable income</th>
          <th scope="col">Tax without relief</th>
          <th scope="col">Tax with relief</th>
          <th scope="col">You keep</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {TAXABLE_ROWS.map((income) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{formatRupees(income)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(withoutRelief(income))}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(tax(income).totalTax)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(income - tax(income).totalTax)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The same band in salary terms</h2>
  <p>
    Taxable income is your salary less the ₹75,000 standard deduction and anything your employer
    puts into NPS for you. With the calculator's standard structure (Basic at half of CTC, PF at the
    wage ceiling, gratuity and employer PF inside the CTC), the relief band covers CTCs from roughly
    13.4 to 14 lakh, and monthly take-home actually falls slightly across it.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">CTC</th>
          <th scope="col">Taxable income</th>
          <th scope="col">Tax and cess</th>
          <th scope="col">In hand a month</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {CTC_ROWS.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{formatRupees(row.ctc)}</td>
            <td>{formatRupees(row.taxable)}</td>
            <td>
              {formatRupees(row.tax)}
              {row.relief ? ' (relief)' : ''}
            </td>
            <td>{formatRupees(row.monthly)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    If a raise lands you in this band, it is worth asking whether part of it can go into employer
    NPS instead: the employer's contribution up to 14% of Basic is deductible under the new regime,
    so it lowers taxable income rather than being taxed at ₹1.04 per rupee.
  </p>

  <h2>Surcharge thresholds work the same way</h2>
  <p>
    A surcharge of 10% starts above ₹50 lakh of taxable income, 15% above ₹1 crore and 25% above ₹2
    crore (the old regime adds 37% above ₹5 crore). At each threshold, tax plus surcharge is capped
    at the tax on the threshold plus the income above it, and the 4% cess is added on top, exactly
    as above.
  </p>

  <h2>The old regime has no such relief</h2>
  <p>
    The old regime's rebate covers taxable income up to ₹5 lakh and stops dead above it. At{' '}
    {formatRupees(5_00_001)} of taxable income the old regime charges{' '}
    {formatRupees(computeIncomeTax('old', 5_00_001).totalTax)}, a genuine cliff that no taper
    softens.
  </p>

  <h2>Why calculators get this wrong</h2>
  <p>
    Many salary calculators treat the rebate as a switch: full rebate up to ₹12 lakh, nothing above.
    Just past the line they then show take-home falling by up to {formatRupees(cliff / 12)} a month,
    as if a raise had made someone poorer. Others describe the relief correctly but forget the cess
    and claim that earning more can never leave you worse off. Both are wrong. This calculator
    applies the cap exactly as section 156(2)(b) reads, and the cess on top of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sources: Income-tax Act, 2025, section 156 (rebate and marginal relief) and section 202 (new
    regime slabs); Finance Act 2026, section 3 (surcharge and the 4% health and education cess).{' '}
    <a href="/how-it-works">Full method</a>.
  </p>
</DocLayout>
